<%#
  One module's status, as a glyph, for the header nav.

  The hub's `_status` partial is the same fact in words on a pill. This is the
  version that fits beside a link in a row of three, and it answers a narrower
  question: is there anything here for me, yes or no. The panel it links to says
  what.

  FOUR states, TWO glyphs. `ready` is the tick; attention, unconfigured and
  unknown are all the question mark, because from the header they mean the same
  thing -- there is something here you have not finished. Splitting them into
  three glyphs would ask somebody to learn a legend to read a nav bar, and the
  distinction they carry is on the panel already.

  `unknown` gets the question mark rather than nothing. A module that failed to
  answer is not a module that is fine, and rendering no glyph would be
  indistinguishable from a console built before this existed.

  Never the colour alone: WCAG 1.4.1. The two glyphs are different SHAPES at any
  size, and the exact state is spelled out for a screen reader below -- so the
  colour is the third signal rather than the only one.
%>
<%= render "sparrow_ui/console/status_glyph", ready: status.ready?, label: status.label %>

<%#
  The word itself, for anything that reads the page aloud. The link's accessible
  name becomes "Auth: Ready" -- it still begins with the visible text, which is
  what WCAG 2.5.3 asks so that "click Auth" keeps working for speech input.
%>
<span class="sr-only">: <%= status.label %></span>
